---
import '../styles/american-morse-block.css';
---

<div class="ia-box">
  <h3>International Morse Code vs American Morse Code</h3>
  <p>
    When people say “Morse code” today, they almost always mean International Morse code. Its older cousin, American
    Morse code, was built for the first telegraph lines in the United States, and the two are not the same alphabet.
  </p>

  <div class="ia-table-wrap">
    <table class="ia-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Feature</th>
          <th scope="col">International Morse</th>
          <th scope="col">American Morse</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr class="ia-r1">
          <td data-label="Feature"><span class="ia-chip">Built for</span></td>
          <td data-label="International Morse" class="ia-a">Radio, ships, aviation, and ham radio</td>
          <td data-label="American Morse" class="ia-b">Landline telegraph wires in the United States</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="ia-r2">
          <td data-label="Feature"><span class="ia-chip">Dash length</span></td>
          <td data-label="International Morse" class="ia-a">One standard dash, three times a dot</td>
          <td data-label="American Morse" class="ia-b">A shorter dash, plus longer dashes for L and 0</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="ia-r3">
          <td data-label="Feature"><span class="ia-chip">Extra pauses inside a letter</span></td>
          <td data-label="International Morse" class="ia-a"
            >None. The gap between signals inside a letter is always one unit</td
          >
          <td data-label="American Morse" class="ia-b"
            >Some letters (C, O, R, Y, Z) have a longer pause in the middle</td
          >
        </tr>
        <tr class="ia-r4">
          <td data-label="Feature"><span class="ia-chip">Still in use?</span></td>
          <td data-label="International Morse" class="ia-a">Yes. It is the worldwide standard (ITU-R M.1677)</td>
          <td data-label="American Morse" class="ia-b"
            >Almost never. You mostly meet it in railroad history and reenactments</td
          >
        </tr>
        <tr class="ia-r5">
          <td data-label="Feature"><span class="ia-chip">Letter C</span></td>
          <td data-label="International Morse" class="ia-a"><span class="ia-code">-.-.</span></td>
          <td data-label="American Morse" class="ia-b"
            ><span class="ia-code">.. .</span> (a pause before the last dot)</td
          >
        </tr>
        <tr class="ia-r1">
          <td data-label="Feature"><span class="ia-chip">Letter L</span></td>
          <td data-label="International Morse" class="ia-a"><span class="ia-code">.-..</span></td>
          <td data-label="American Morse" class="ia-b">One long dash</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="ia-r2">
          <td data-label="Feature"><span class="ia-chip">Letter O</span></td>
          <td data-label="International Morse" class="ia-a"><span class="ia-code">---</span></td>
          <td data-label="American Morse" class="ia-b"><span class="ia-code">. .</span> (two dots with a pause)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="ia-r3">
          <td data-label="Feature"><span class="ia-chip">Letter R</span></td>
          <td data-label="International Morse" class="ia-a"><span class="ia-code">.-.</span></td>
          <td data-label="American Morse" class="ia-b"
            ><span class="ia-code">. ..</span> (a pause after the first dot)</td
          >
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Which one should you use?</h3>
  <p>
    Use International Morse code. It is the standard for radio, aviation, and learning, and it is the code this
    translator uses. Radio signals cross borders, so ships and stations needed one shared code, and an international
    radio conference in London named it for radio messages in 1912. American Morse only matters if you are reading old
    telegraph records or taking part in railroad history events.
  </p>

  <div class="ia-note">
    <strong>Quick tip:</strong> If a Morse message was written for an old American telegraph line, it will not decode
    correctly as International Morse. Check which code it uses before you translate.
  </div>
</div>
